module txnd-criminal-local-rules.local-rules

import core.authority.{ CaseOrder }
import core.frcrimp45.{ frcrimp45 }

// The Northern District of Texas's own criminal periods, counted under the
// criminal rules (FRCrimP 45(a) governs "any local rule"). No trigger
// carries a service method: FRCrimP 45(c)'s three added days follow the
// manner of service, which is not modeled here. A paper filed by
// electronic means before midnight central time is deemed filed that day,
// and one filed on paper before the clerk's office is scheduled to close
// (LCrR 45.1).
//
// The objections to a magistrate judge's order or recommendation are
// FRCrimP 59(a) and 59(b)(2)'s periods and stay in lit/frcrimp; this layer
// adds only the response brief (LCrR 59.1(b), 59.2(b)). A reply brief to a
// motion or to objections needs leave and is due when the presiding judge
// sets (LCrR 47.1(f), 59.1(c), 59.2(c)), so no reply period is encoded.
// Motion practice in a § 2241, § 2254 or § 2255 proceeding follows the
// local civil rules (LCrR 47.4); federal habeas belongs to lit/habeas.
//
// Conditions that are not deadlines from an event stay in notes: the
// probation officer does not disclose a sentencing recommendation (LCrR
// 32.1); jurors are not contacted before or after trial unless the judge
// permits (LCrR 24.1); a motion carries a certificate of conference unless
// LCrR 47.1(a) or (h) excuses it; an informally granted extension is
// confirmed by a filed document (LCrR 47.3); the signer of a paper with
// another person's electronic signature keeps the signed paper copy for
// one year after final disposition of the case (LCrR 49.5(d)); a change
// of contact information is made promptly (LCrR 57.13). Courtroom
// conduct, case files, attorney admission and discipline (LCrR 53.1 to
// 57.14) state no period of the criminal case.
//
// The earlier N.D. Tex. criminal outline's FRCrimP flow and its decisions
// (United States v. Salerno, Brady v. Maryland, Barker v. Wingo, Zedner v.
// United States, Boykin v. Alabama, Missouri v. Frye, Jackson v. Virginia,
// United States v. Booker, Roe v. Flores-Ortega, Garza v. Idaho) are
// FRCrimP, Speedy Trial Act or constitutional authority and belong to
// lit/frcrimp; they are removed here: no reporter text is vendored.

/// A brief, including a brief supporting objections to a magistrate
/// judge's ruling: at most 25 pages, excluding the tables of contents and
/// authorities; the presiding judge permits more only for extraordinary
/// and compelling reasons (N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.2(c); LCrR 59.1, 59.2).
limit brief {
  pages <= 25 cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.2(c)"
} cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.2(c)"

/// A reply brief: at most 10 pages (N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.2(c)).
limit reply-brief {
  pages <= 10 cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.2(c)"
} cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.2(c)"

/// The scheduled date for trial (N.D. Tex. LCrR 16.1, 23.1, 30.1).
event TrialScheduled()
/// An opposed motion with its brief is filed (N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.1(d),
/// (e)).
event OpposedMotionFiled() paper brief
/// An attorney appears in a case (N.D. Tex. LCrR 49.2(g)).
event AttorneyAppeared()
/// A charging document, or a document signed by a criminal defendant, is
/// submitted by electronic means (N.D. Tex. LCrR 49.6).
event SignedDocumentSubmitted()
/// The case is finally disposed of (N.D. Tex. LCrR 55.2(b), 55.4).
event FinalDisposition()
/// Objections to a magistrate judge's order on a nondispositive matter,
/// or to a recommendation on a dispositive motion, are filed with their
/// brief (N.D. Tex. LCrR 59.1(a), 59.2(a)).
event MagistrateObjectionsFiled() paper brief

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 16.1(a), (b): at least 14 days before the scheduled
/// date for trial, the parties exchange the marked exhibits they intend to
/// offer, other than impeachment exhibits, and file and deliver to the
/// opposing parties and the court reporter separate lists of exhibits and
/// witnesses.
deadline exhibit-exchange: 14.days before TrialScheduled() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 16.1"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 23.1: unless the presiding judge directs otherwise, at
/// least 14 days before trial in a nonjury case the parties file and serve
/// proposed findings of fact and conclusions of law.
deadline proposed-findings: 14.days before TrialScheduled() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 23.1"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 30.1: unless the presiding judge directs otherwise, at
/// least 14 days before trial each party files and serves its requested
/// jury charge, including instructions.
deadline requested-jury-charge: 14.days before TrialScheduled() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 30.1"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.1(e): a response and brief to an opposed motion are
/// filed within 14 days from the date the motion is filed, unless the
/// presiding judge directs otherwise (LCrR 47.1).
deadline response-period: 14.days after OpposedMotionFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 47.1(e)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 49.2(g): unless excused for cause, an attorney other
/// than a prisoner pro se party registers as an ECF user within 14 days of
/// the date the attorney appears in a case.
deadline ecf-registration: 14.days after AttorneyAppeared() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 49.2(g)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 49.6: the attorney who submits a charging document or a
/// document signed by a criminal defendant by electronic means delivers an
/// original, signed paper document to the clerk within 7 days.
deadline paper-original: 7.days after SignedDocumentSubmitted() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 49.6"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 55.2(b): every exhibit in the court's custody is
/// removed from the clerk's office, by the attorney who introduced it,
/// within 60 days after final disposition of the case; one not removed may
/// be destroyed or otherwise disposed of by the clerk.
deadline exhibit-removal: 60.days after FinalDisposition() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 55.2(b)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 55.4: unless the presiding judge directs otherwise, a
/// sealed document kept on paper is deemed unsealed 60 days after final
/// disposition of the case; a party that wants it kept sealed moves for
/// that relief before the 60-day period expires.
deadline keep-sealed-motion: 60.days after FinalDisposition() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 55.4"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D. Tex. LCrR 59.1(b), 59.2(b): a response brief to objections filed
/// under FRCrimP 59(a) or 59(b)(2) complies with LCrR 47.2 and is filed
/// within 14 days from the date the objections are filed.
deadline magistrate-objection-response: 14.days after MagistrateObjectionsFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D. Tex. LCrR 59.1(b); N.D. Tex. LCrR 59.2(b)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}
